T.push(
{id:"stoicism",n:10,grp:"greek",name:"Stoicism",who:"Epictetus, Seneca, Marcus Aurelius",era:"Greece & Rome, 3rd c. BCE – 2nd c. CE",
cause:"False judgements: treating externals like jobs, money and reputation as if they were genuinely good or bad.",
interp:"Epictetus — himself born a slave — begins the Handbook by dividing things into what is up to us (judgements, intentions, desires) and what is not (body, property, reputation, office). The job was never up to you. What disturbs you, he says, is not things but your judgements about them. For the Stoic, the job and the salary are 'preferred indifferents': reasonable to pursue, but not part of your goodness. Seneca adds practical advice: rehearse poverty so you stop fearing it. Marcus reminds himself that everything is change and that the obstacle becomes the way.",
voice:"I did not choose to lose my job. But I can choose how I respond. My character is not determined by my professional position. Losing a title costs me nothing that was truly mine.",
advice:["Sort each worry: up to me, or not? Act only on the first list.","Practise premeditatio malorum: imagine further setbacks calmly so they lose their power.","Follow Seneca: spend a few days living very cheaply to prove poverty is survivable.","Keep a nightly review: what did I do well, badly, and what is left undone?","Apply for work as a duty, with a 'reserve clause': I'll pursue this, if nothing prevents it."],
aim:"Virtue (aretē), living in agreement with nature and reason; the result is a tranquil, stable mind.",
insight:"It is not events but our judgements about them that disturb us.",
diff:"Stoicism is more rational and effortful than Taoism, and more individual than Confucianism. It agrees with Aristotle that virtue matters most, but denies Aristotle's view that external goods are needed to flourish. It is the direct ancestor of CBT (Ellis and Beck cite Epictetus). Unlike ACT, it does aim to change judgements, not merely to hold them lightly.",
deep:"<b>Terms.</b> <i>Prohairesis</i>: faculty of choice. <i>Apatheia</i>: freedom from destructive passions, not emotional numbness. <i>Preferred indifferents</i>: health, wealth, status. <b>Original vs modern.</b> Ancient Stoicism was a full system of physics, logic and ethics with a providential, rational cosmos. Modern 'Stoicism' often drops that and becomes a toolkit for resilience or productivity, sometimes with a macho tone the ancients would not recognise.",
refs:["Epictetus, Discourses, Fragments, Handbook, trans. Robin Hard (Oxford, 2014)","Seneca, Letters on Ethics, trans. Graver & Long (Chicago, 2015) — esp. Letter 18","Marcus Aurelius, Meditations, trans. Robin Hard or Gregory Hays","A.A. Long, Epictetus: A Stoic and Socratic Guide to Life (OUP, 2002)","Pierre Hadot, The Inner Citadel (Harvard, 1998)"]},

{id:"epicurus",n:11,grp:"greek",name:"Epicureanism",who:"Epicurus (and Lucretius)",era:"Athens, 4th–3rd c. BCE",
cause:"Empty, unnatural desires (status, wealth, fame) and baseless fears.",
interp:"Epicurus is often misread as a hedonist of luxury. In fact he taught that the highest pleasure is ataraxia — untroubled calm — and aponia, absence of bodily pain. He sorted desires into natural and necessary (food, shelter, friendship), natural but unnecessary (fine food), and empty (fame, power). The job gave you money for necessities, which matters, but much of the distress may come from empty desires: status, being seen as successful. He would point you toward friends, simple needs and withdrawal from the race.",
voice:"What do I actually need? Food, a roof, a few friends, time to think. The anxiety about prestige isn't hunger; it's a desire I picked up somewhere. Bread and water, shared with a friend, would be a decent day.",
advice:["List your desires and sort them using Epicurus's three categories.","Cut costs to necessities so that fear of poverty shrinks.","Spend time with close friends — Epicurus held friendship to be among the greatest goods.","Avoid the anxiety of public life; seek work that gives sufficiency and calm, not glory.","Use the 'fourfold remedy' as a reminder: the gods are not to be feared, death is nothing, the good is easy to get, the terrible is easy to endure."],
aim:"Ataraxia: a tranquil, pleasant life among friends.",
insight:"Most of what we fear losing, we never needed.",
diff:"Unlike Stoicism, pleasure (rightly understood) is the good, not virtue. Unlike Aristotle, Epicurus counsels withdrawal from public honour. He shares with Buddhism a critique of desire, but does not aim to end all desire, only the empty ones, and he denies any afterlife.",
deep:"<b>Terms.</b> <i>Ataraxia</i>: tranquillity. <i>Tetrapharmakos</i>: fourfold remedy (a later summary, attributed to Philodemus). <i>Lathe biōsas</i>: 'live unnoticed'. <b>Note.</b> Most of Epicurus's writing is lost; we rely on three letters, the Principal Doctrines and Lucretius's poem.",
refs:["Epicurus, Letter to Menoeceus and Principal Doctrines, in The Epicurus Reader, ed. Inwood & Gerson (Hackett, 1994)","Lucretius, On the Nature of Things, trans. Martin Ferguson Smith (Hackett)","Catherine Wilson, How to Be an Epicurean (2019)","Tim O'Keefe, Epicureanism (Acumen, 2010)"]},

{id:"aristotle",n:12,grp:"greek",name:"Aristotelianism",who:"Aristotle; eudaimonia",era:"Athens, 4th c. BCE",
cause:"Not misjudgement alone: the real loss of a good — meaningful activity, a sphere for one's excellences.",
interp:"Aristotle would say you are right to feel the loss. Eudaimonia — flourishing — is activity of the soul in accordance with virtue over a complete life, and it requires some external goods: friends, resources, a role in the community. Work that exercises your capacities is part of a good life. So the job loss genuinely harms you, though it doesn't ruin you. He also says a virtuous person bears misfortune with nobility, and that fortune can tip the balance. The question becomes: what activities will now exercise your best capacities, and what habits must you keep while you look?",
voice:"I lost something real: a place to use my skills. Grieving that is right, not weakness. But flourishing is a whole life, not one year. What are my excellences, and where can I use them next?",
advice:["Identify your characteristic excellences and the activities that use them.","Keep virtuous habits — courage, temperance, practical wisdom — through daily routine.","Lean on friendships of character (philia), which Aristotle treats as essential to flourishing.","Aim at the mean: neither despair nor reckless bravado about money.","Get involved in civic or communal life; humans are political animals."],
aim:"Eudaimonia: flourishing through excellent activity over a complete life.",
insight:"A good life is an activity, practised with others, and fortune genuinely matters to it.",
diff:"This is the key disagreement with Stoicism: for Aristotle, externals affect happiness; for the Stoics, they don't. Aristotle shares Confucianism's emphasis on habituated virtue and community. Positive psychology draws on him, often loosely.",
deep:"<b>Terms.</b> <i>Eudaimonia</i>: flourishing, living well. <i>Aretē</i>: excellence. <i>Phronēsis</i>: practical wisdom. <i>Mesotēs</i>: the mean. <b>Tension.</b> Book X of the Ethics ranks contemplation above the practical life, which sits awkwardly with the rest.",
refs:["Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics, trans. Terence Irwin (Hackett) or Roger Crisp (CUP) — esp. I.7–10","Martha Nussbaum, The Fragility of Goodness (CUP, 1986)","Richard Kraut, Aristotle on the Human Good (Princeton, 1989)"]},

{id:"cynicism",n:13,grp:"greek",name:"Cynicism",who:"Diogenes of Sinope",era:"Athens & Corinth, 4th c. BCE",
cause:"Convention (nomos): living by society's fake values of status, property and propriety.",
interp:"Diogenes lived in a large jar, owned almost nothing, and mocked Athenian respectability. A story has him throwing away his cup on seeing a child drink from cupped hands. To a Cynic, your distress is evidence of how deeply you've swallowed convention: the idea that a job title makes you worth something. He'd find it hilarious that you feel ashamed of being freed from it. His prescription: strip away the unnecessary, live according to nature, and say plainly what is true.",
voice:"Shame? Why? Because a company stopped paying me to sit in meetings? I'm the same person I was on Friday. If the opinions of people who value titles hurt, maybe the opinions are the problem.",
advice:["Practise voluntary hardship (askēsis): cold showers, simple food, walking instead of transport.","Examine one convention you obey only out of fear of judgement.","Speak with frankness (parrhēsia) about your situation instead of hiding it.","Reduce possessions and spending to see how little you need.","Consider whether you want a conventional career at all."],
aim:"Self-sufficiency (autarkeia) and freedom through living according to nature.",
insight:"Most of what we are ashamed of is convention, not nature.",
diff:"Cynicism is the most confrontational tradition here; Stoicism grew out of it but kept social duties. It shares with Taoism a suspicion of civilisation, but is aggressive and public where Taoism is quiet. Its rejection of work as such would be resisted by Confucius and Aristotle.",
deep:"<b>Note.</b> Diogenes wrote nothing that survives; we know him from anecdotes, mainly Diogenes Laertius, written centuries later. Modern 'cynicism' (distrust) is unrelated in meaning.",
refs:["Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, Book VI, trans. Pamela Mensch (OUP, 2018)","Robin Hard (trans.), Diogenes the Cynic: Sayings and Anecdotes (Oxford, 2012)","Luis Navia, Diogenes the Cynic (2005)"]},

{id:"plato",n:14,grp:"greek",name:"Platonism",who:"Plato (through Socrates)",era:"Athens, 4th c. BCE",
cause:"Disordered soul; confusing appearances (wealth, honour) with what is truly good.",
interp:"Plato would place your situation in the allegory of the cave: we take shadows on the wall — status, money, applause — for reality. In the Republic, the soul has reason, spirit (honour) and appetite. A job that fed the spirited part's hunger for recognition might have been making the soul's order worse, not better. Socrates in the Apology says the unexamined life is not worth living for a human being. Losing the job might be an invitation to turn toward the Good: to ask what is actually worth living for.",
voice:"Was I pursuing the good, or its reflection? The applause, the title, the salary — these were the shadows. What would I do with my life if I cared about my soul more than my reputation?",
advice:["Engage in dialectic: talk through your beliefs with a friend who will question them.","Ask what each desire is for, and keep asking until you reach something good in itself.","Order the soul: let reason govern honour and appetite in your choices.","Study what lasts — philosophy, mathematics, beauty — as a form of orientation.","Choose work that fits your nature and serves the common good (the Republic's division of labour)."],
aim:"A just, well-ordered soul oriented toward the Form of the Good.",
insight:"What we chase is often a shadow of what we actually want.",
diff:"Plato is more metaphysical than Aristotle, who brings the good down into activity. He shares with Advaita and Christian Neoplatonism the idea of a higher reality. Existentialists reject his fixed essences.",
deep:"<b>Terms.</b> <i>Forms</i>: perfect, unchanging realities. <i>Thumos</i>: spirited part. <b>Note.</b> 'Socrates' in Plato's dialogues is partly a literary character; early dialogues are often thought to be closer to the historical Socrates.",
refs:["Plato, Republic, trans. G.M.A. Grube rev. C.D.C. Reeve (Hackett) — Books IV, VII","Plato, Apology","Julia Annas, An Introduction to Plato's Republic (OUP, 1981)"]},

{id:"skeptic",n:15,grp:"greek",name:"Skepticism",who:"Pyrrho & Sextus Empiricus",era:"Greece, 4th c. BCE – 2nd c. CE",
cause:"Dogmatic belief that things are by nature good or bad.",
interp:"Pyrrhonian Skeptics noticed that for every argument there seems to be an equal counter-argument. Suspending judgement (epochē) led, to their surprise, to tranquillity. Applied here: you are distressed because you believe, firmly, that losing the job is bad, that you've wasted potential, that the future is bleak. Do you know any of that? Sextus says the Skeptic still suffers the hunger and cold that come unbidden, but is spared the extra layer of believing it's terrible by nature.",
voice:"Is this bad? I don't actually know. It might be; it might not be. I can still feel worried, but I don't need to add the certainty that my life has gone wrong.",
advice:["For each catastrophic belief, build the strongest counter-argument.","Suspend judgement where evidence is balanced.","Follow appearances and customs for practical life: apply for jobs, eat, sleep.","Notice the relief when you stop needing to be sure."],
aim:"Ataraxia through epochē: tranquillity by suspending dogmatic belief.",
insight:"We suffer twice: once from the event, again from the certainty that it is bad.",
diff:"Skepticism removes belief rather than correcting it, unlike Stoicism and CBT. It shares with Zhuangzi a relativism about perspectives, and with ACT the idea of not being fused with thoughts.",
deep:"<b>Terms.</b> <i>Epochē</i>: suspension of judgement. <i>Isostheneia</i>: equal strength of opposing arguments. <b>Note.</b> Academic Skepticism (Arcesilaus, Carneades) is a different school. Pyrrho left no writings.",
refs:["Sextus Empiricus, Outlines of Scepticism, trans. Annas & Barnes (CUP, 2000)","Richard Bett, Pyrrho, His Antecedents and His Legacy (OUP, 2000)","Martha Nussbaum, The Therapy of Desire (Princeton, 1994)"]}
);
